### Software Installation

# What I learn today.
To install any software packages or services we need a package manager. A package manager takes care of the software packages.

1. Red Hat Package Manager (RPM)
-Whenever a package is install or manage it is done in /var/lib/rpm.
- rpm -ihv install packagename (don't install the dependencies which are not already available)
-They are specific to the distro.
- rpm check the package and all it dependencies.
- rpm -i --test <packagename>
- If missing some dependecies then it will so and error and we can install that package.

2. Yellowdog Updater, Modified (YUM)
- Made for redhat and specific distro but we can use zypper for open SUSE
- yum install packagename (install the package and all it dependencis)
- yumdownloader <packagename> this download the package but not install it.
- yum list installed packagename to see the package
- There is another package manager call dnf work same as yum but used for more distro.
- dnf list - to see the packages.
- dnf erase <package name> - to delete

3. Debian Package Manager (dpkg)
- command = dpkg option <action> <package_name>
- dpkg -i packagename - install
- dpkg -p packagename - view the package
- dpkg -r packagename - this will uninstall the package but it will not the dependencis are weather affected to other files or not soooo....
- apt -r packagename - this check the dependence and the uninstall safly
- dpkg -l to list the all packages.
